00:00:02.929 --> 00:00:06.349 align:start position:0%
what is the council presidency and how  does it work the council presidency is  responsible for driving forward the  council's work each member state is  called upon in turn to take on this role  and ensure continuity in the council's  agenda the council presidency has  several key tasks it pans and chairs  meetings of the ministers and working  groups of civil servants discussing EU  legislation in the council but in the  case of Foreign Affairs it is the EU  High Representative for foreign and  security policy who plans and chairs  meetings the presidency also represents  the council in relations with the  Commission and the European Parliament  it negotiates on behalf of the council  to reach agreement on legislative files  with the other EU institutions the  presidency of the Council rotates among  the EU Member States every six months  this means that each country takes it in  terms to leave the council and prepare a  six-month work programme the order of  rotation of the presidencies is set by  the council member states holding the  presidency work together in groups of  three called trios they set long-term  goals and prepare a common agenda of  topics to be addressed by the council  over an 18-month period these long term  goals are guided by the priorities  adopted by the European Council in its  strategic agenda covering for instance  jobs growth and competitiveness energy  and climate policies freedom security  and justice find out when your country  will next be leading the council and  much more on Concilium europa dot EU  [Music]
 

